1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
ROBERT STACK, 1991, tanks speed across a barren Saudi desert

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
on their way to Kuwait.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
But how did this massive strike force get water,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
food, and shelter in the middle of a desert?

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
1968, a wounded soldier in Vietnam

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
is airlifted to a field hospital only moments away.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
But who cut through the dense jungle to build it?

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,000
It was killed it.

9
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:36,500
1945, D-Day, the largest amphibious force ever assembled

10
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:39,000
lands at Normandy.

11
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:45,000
But how did they get through millions of mines and miles of obstacles?

12
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:48,000
The answer in each of these cases is the same.

13
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:51,500
It involves soldiers trained in what is perhaps the most overlooked

14
00:00:51,501 --> 00:00:55,000
and unsung aspect of warfare.

15
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:58,000
Next on Modern Marvels, Battlefield Engineering.

16
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:00,000
engineering.

17
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:05,000
ELIZABETH WOOD

18
00:01:23,157 --> 00:01:25,000
FITLEEN Because preparing for battle

19
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:27,900
didn't usually have the drama of the battle it's

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,500
Most historians, novelists and movie makers

21
00:01:31,501 --> 00:01:34,500
focused on the fighting rather than the actions

22
00:01:34,501 --> 00:01:36,500
preceding the fighting.

23
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,500
But history shows in the world of battlefield engineering,

24
00:01:40,501 --> 00:01:45,500
there was more than enough drama and fighting to go around.

25
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:51,500
It has been the job of the battlefield engineer

26
00:01:51,501 --> 00:01:55,500
to lay the groundwork, quite literally, for the battle to come.

27
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
They built roads, railroads and bridges.

28
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:07,000
They constructed airfields, barracks, hospitals, supply depots,

29
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:12,000
and all the other facilities that supported combat operations.

30
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:15,000
They were earthmovers and river crossers.

31
00:02:15,001 --> 00:02:18,000
They built things and they destroyed things.

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Their mission was critical because without basis from which to operate,

33
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:30,000
there was no way to mount an attack.

34
00:02:30,001 --> 00:02:34,000
Without open supply lines, an army would quickly run out of food,

35
00:02:34,001 --> 00:02:36,600
water, and ammunition.

36
00:02:36,601 --> 00:02:41,100
The Navy's engineers, the C.Bs, have a slogan.

37
00:02:41,101 --> 00:02:45,100
We build, we fight.

38
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:48,500
When I'm asked what the CBs do, the answer to the short answer

39
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:52,500
is we do anything and everything, and we do it quickly.

40
00:02:52,501 --> 00:02:56,000
Ask an Army engineer, and the answer will sound much the same.

41
00:02:56,001 --> 00:02:58,000
Remember one thing.

42
00:02:58,001 --> 00:03:01,000
The engineers are the ones who get them in

43
00:03:01,001 --> 00:03:03,000
and they wanted to get them out.

44
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:06,000
And if you don't believe me, just ask an infantryman.

45
00:03:06,001 --> 00:03:07,000
They'll tell you.

46
00:03:07,001 --> 00:03:12,000
Throughout history, man has built fortifications.

47
00:03:12,001 --> 00:03:17,000
Building those fortifications and breaking through them

48
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:21,000
gave rise to the first battlefield engineers.

49
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:24,000
There's a dichotomy between warriors and soldiers.

50
00:03:24,001 --> 00:03:27,000
soldiers. Warriors are interested in defeating individuals.

51
00:03:27,001 --> 00:03:30,000
They're not interested in conquering a province,

52
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:31,000
defeating an entire army.

53
00:03:31,001 --> 00:03:33,000
They don't care what's going on next to them.

54
00:03:33,001 --> 00:03:35,000
They care about the one person they're fighting.

55
00:03:35,001 --> 00:03:38,000
A soldier is part of a unit, which is part of a greater unit,

56
00:03:38,001 --> 00:03:39,000
which is part of an army.

57
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:41,000
He fights as a member of a collective.

58
00:03:41,001 --> 00:03:44,000
He has greater goals.

59
00:03:44,001 --> 00:03:46,000
And this is perhaps the most profound difference.

60
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:51,000
Military engineering flows out of soldiers as opposed to warriors.

61
00:03:51,001 --> 00:03:53,000
Warriors are not military engineers.

62
00:03:53,001 --> 00:03:55,000
Soldiers are.

63
00:03:55,001 --> 00:04:07,000
It is strangely ironic that the same engineers who are called upon to devise a defense are often later called upon to devise a way to break through it.

64
00:04:07,001 --> 00:04:10,000
The fort begets the battering ram.

65
00:04:10,001 --> 00:04:16,000
The more heavily fortified castle begets the siege engine and the catapult.

66
00:04:16,001 --> 00:04:22,000
As defenses strengthen, they require bigger, stronger, more lethal ways to penetrate them.

67
00:04:22,001 --> 00:04:25,000
them.

68
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:29,900
Over 2,000 years ago, the Romans decided that if they were going to rule the world, they needed

69
00:04:29,901 --> 00:04:31,780
a way to get there.

70
00:04:31,781 --> 00:04:38,820
Some of their roads and bridges were so well constructed they remained to this day.

71
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:44,460
Following the fall of the Roman Empire, in the Dark Ages and the Middle Ages, defenders built

72
00:04:44,461 --> 00:04:50,560
better and bigger walls, causing attackers to go over or under them.

73
00:04:50,561 --> 00:04:52,760
To counter the wall, to counter the defenses

74
00:04:52,761 --> 00:04:55,800
that the engineers would come up with, then,

75
00:04:55,801 --> 00:04:59,200
machines were created, one of them being the catapult,

76
00:04:59,201 --> 00:05:01,400
a way to get over the wall.

77
00:05:01,401 --> 00:05:02,600
You threw a rock.

78
00:05:02,601 --> 00:05:04,800
In some cases, you threw a dead horse,

79
00:05:04,801 --> 00:05:09,320
or you threw a dead body to spread disease.

80
00:05:09,321 --> 00:05:12,620
Another way of dealing with walls was to breach them.

81
00:05:12,621 --> 00:05:17,320
This was usually done by soldiers called sappers and miners.

82
00:05:17,321 --> 00:05:19,860
Saffers would dig the trenches above the battlefield,

83
00:05:19,861 --> 00:05:21,860
and they would trench up to the enemy fortifications,

84
00:05:21,861 --> 00:05:24,360
and at the last minute, a group of volunteers

85
00:05:24,361 --> 00:05:27,360
and as a forlorn hope would attack the fortification walls

86
00:05:27,361 --> 00:05:30,360
and destroy them to breach to the soft underbelly inside.

87
00:05:30,361 --> 00:05:32,860
Now, the miners would tunnel underneath the fortifications

88
00:05:32,861 --> 00:05:34,360
to collapse the enemy's walls

89
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:36,360
and breach from the underneath side.

90
00:05:36,361 --> 00:05:39,360
Castles were having mines dug under their walls

91
00:05:39,361 --> 00:05:44,360
with such frequency that there arose a need for mine detectors.

92
00:05:44,361 --> 00:05:47,360
Mind detection can go back as far as the Middle Ages,

93
00:05:47,361 --> 00:05:49,360
when women would carry big copper bowls

94
00:05:49,361 --> 00:05:51,120
copper bowls filled with water down to the basement

95
00:05:51,121 --> 00:05:54,500
of the castle or the fortification, set it on the stone floor

96
00:05:54,501 --> 00:05:58,400
and be domestic down there, sewing, cooking,

97
00:05:58,401 --> 00:05:59,400
but keeping an eye on the water.

98
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:02,900
And when they'd see ripples developing in the surface of the water,

99
00:06:02,901 --> 00:06:04,300
they'd say, I detect a mine.

100
00:06:04,301 --> 00:06:06,720
I detect the vibrations of the picks and the shovels

101
00:06:06,721 --> 00:06:08,620
of the miners that are undermining our foundation

102
00:06:08,621 --> 00:06:11,580
to collapse our walls.

103
00:06:11,581 --> 00:06:15,020
As fortifications became virtually impenetrable,

104
00:06:15,021 --> 00:06:17,820
sieges became less effective.

105
00:06:17,821 --> 00:06:19,260
But then, in the 14th and 15th,

106
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:22,600
15th centuries, the introduction of gunpowder

107
00:06:22,601 --> 00:06:26,600
and cannons changed battlefields forever.

108
00:06:26,601 --> 00:06:29,700
If you have a castle and you could keep your opponent

109
00:06:29,701 --> 00:06:32,800
in the field for months, your opponent would end up starving

110
00:06:32,801 --> 00:06:33,800
outside your walls.

111
00:06:33,801 --> 00:06:34,800
Winter would come.

112
00:06:34,801 --> 00:06:37,120
The opponent would not be able to provision his armies,

113
00:06:37,121 --> 00:06:39,120
and you won it.

114
00:06:39,121 --> 00:06:41,620
When Gunpowder came along, the opponent knocks down your wall.

115
00:06:41,621 --> 00:06:43,820
He marches by you, and he wins.

116
00:06:43,821 --> 00:06:48,660
Essentially, Gunpowder made the castle obsolete.

117
00:06:48,661 --> 00:06:50,460
Following the death of the castle,

118
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:54,240
the innovative French military engineer Sebastian Vaubain

119
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:58,440
proposed a radically new type of fortification.

120
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:03,480
Thick walls low to the ground helped protect from cannon fire.

121
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:07,180
A star-shaped design allowed defenders inside the fort

122
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:10,480
to fire at attackers from virtually any angle.

123
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:14,320
By the time of the American Revolutionary War,

124
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:18,660
the monumental changes in battlefield tactics caused by the use,

125
00:07:18,661 --> 00:07:22,060
use of gunpowder and cannons had completely changed

126
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:23,360
how wars were fought.

127
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:28,560
An army had to have trained and educated battlefield engineers.

128
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:31,400
In the American Revolution, the army was created,

129
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:33,500
all dressed up to go somewhere off the fight,

130
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,820
and found out, gee, guys were missing something.

131
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:36,920
We have no engineers.

132
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:41,620
Congress says, let us have some, let us promote some people

133
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,320
to be engineers.

134
00:07:43,321 --> 00:07:44,120
No, you don't understand.

135
00:07:44,220 --> 00:07:45,360
We have no engineers.

136
00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:46,960
We have to hire them.

137
00:07:46,961 --> 00:07:54,960
In 1775, George Washington created what was to become the Army Corps of Engineers.

138
00:07:54,961 --> 00:07:59,960
And it was fortuitous that the French, perhaps the best engineers in the world at the time,

139
00:07:59,961 --> 00:08:04,960
were more than happy to supply engineers to help defeat the English.

140
00:08:04,961 --> 00:08:11,960
After the war in 1802, Thomas Jefferson created the nation's first engineering school

141
00:08:11,961 --> 00:08:14,960
at a fort located at West Point, New York.

142
00:08:14,961 --> 00:08:15,960
It would be the nation's primary primary school.

143
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:19,560
nation's primary source of engineers and military officers

144
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:21,260
for decades to come.

145
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:28,700
In 1861, the American Civil War introduced

146
00:08:28,701 --> 00:08:31,700
the biggest revolution in battlefield engineering

147
00:08:31,701 --> 00:08:34,820
since gunpowder, railroads.

148
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:39,220
The ability to quickly transport large numbers of troops

149
00:08:39,221 --> 00:08:43,820
to wherever they were needed changed military tactics dramatically.

150
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:48,960
The Northern armies controlled 30,000 miles

151
00:08:48,961 --> 00:08:52,460
of America's railroads, more track that could be found

152
00:08:52,461 --> 00:08:55,960
in the rest of the world, and they continued laying

153
00:08:55,961 --> 00:08:59,960
even more rail, even as they ripped up hundreds of miles

154
00:08:59,961 --> 00:09:01,960
of Confederate track.

155
00:09:01,961 --> 00:09:04,460
The Union Army in the North had the industrial ability

156
00:09:04,461 --> 00:09:06,960
to manufacture locomotives and extend rail lines

157
00:09:06,961 --> 00:09:09,960
at a prodigious rate, while the Confederate Army

158
00:09:09,961 --> 00:09:11,960
in the South lacked that ability.

159
00:09:11,961 --> 00:09:14,960
Railroads were being destroyed, and locomotives burned

160
00:09:14,961 --> 00:09:17,260
much faster than they could be replaced in the south,

161
00:09:17,261 --> 00:09:21,460
creating a tremendous disadvantage for southern mobility.

162
00:09:21,461 --> 00:09:24,460
Railroad bridges were built and rebuilt again

163
00:09:24,461 --> 00:09:26,800
and again during this war.

164
00:09:26,801 --> 00:09:29,500
This one was rebuilt seven times.

165
00:09:29,501 --> 00:09:33,420
When Abraham Lincoln saw this bridge over the Potomac,

166
00:09:33,421 --> 00:09:35,020
he said it looked like it had been made

167
00:09:35,021 --> 00:09:38,220
from bean poles and corn stalks.

168
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:41,020
This bridge over the Chattahoochee River

169
00:09:41,021 --> 00:09:44,860
was 800 feet long, nearly 100 feet high.

170
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,160
It took only four and a half days to build,

171
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:52,100
an astounding accomplishment in that or any time period.

172
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:57,380
And timber bridges weren't the only record-breaking projects.

173
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:01,800
The most significant military engineering development in the Civil War

174
00:10:01,801 --> 00:10:05,020
was the advent of the potten or pontoon bridge.

175
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:10,320
Bridges built across boats or ships date as early as the 5th century BC.

176
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:12,580
But for us, it was a new challenge.

177
00:10:12,581 --> 00:10:17,580
Engineers responded to that challenge to the point that in 1864,

178
00:10:17,581 --> 00:10:22,580
they built a 2,200-foot float bridge across the James River

179
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:24,580
in slightly more than 10 hours.

180
00:10:24,581 --> 00:10:28,580
That's the longest American float bridge built by the engineers

181
00:10:28,581 --> 00:10:30,580
in American history.

182
00:10:30,581 --> 00:10:34,580
The success and the rapid capture of old-style forts

183
00:10:34,581 --> 00:10:37,580
signaled the beginning of the end of fixed masonry forts.

184
00:10:37,581 --> 00:10:41,580
They were replaced by massive earthwork fortifications,

185
00:10:41,581 --> 00:10:45,320
In the future, trenches would replace walls

186
00:10:45,420 --> 00:10:47,620
as the dominant type of defense.

187
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:51,720
The widespread use of mortars and cannons

188
00:10:51,820 --> 00:10:56,320
on railroad cars heralded a new, more mobile use of heavy artillery.

189
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:00,340
On a smaller but still ominous note,

190
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:03,240
another innovation appeared that would drastically

191
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,040
alter ground warfare.

192
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:09,740
In 1862, we have a Confederate engineer officer

193
00:11:09,741 --> 00:11:11,240
who will put a few,

194
00:11:11,580 --> 00:11:16,580
on an artillery shell and bury that shell in a road.

195
00:11:16,581 --> 00:11:19,080
When the Union Cavalry comes along,

196
00:11:19,081 --> 00:11:21,080
the shell detonates.

197
00:11:21,081 --> 00:11:23,580
That's the first use in the American Army

198
00:11:23,581 --> 00:11:27,580
of what we would refer to today as a landmine.

199
00:11:27,581 --> 00:11:30,580
The battlefield innovations witnessed during the Civil War

200
00:11:30,581 --> 00:11:33,580
provided a glimpse into the future.

201
00:11:33,581 --> 00:11:37,580
A half century later, a war they would call the Great War

202
00:11:37,581 --> 00:11:41,080
would continue a trend toward a more entrenched, more horridged,

203
00:11:41,081 --> 00:11:43,080
more horrific kind of combat.

204
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:50,080
Battlefield engineering will continue in a moment here on the History Channel.

205
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:57,080
We now return to Battlefield Engineering.

206
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:05,080
World War I was called the War to end all wars,

207
00:12:05,081 --> 00:12:10,080
but it might better have been called the war to begin all modern wars.

208
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:20,080
When the fighting began in Europe in the summer of 1914,

209
00:12:20,081 --> 00:12:24,080
advances in the technology of killing took more soldiers' lives

210
00:12:24,081 --> 00:12:28,080
in less time than ever before.

211
00:12:28,081 --> 00:12:33,080
The world had never seen anything like the trenches of World War I,

212
00:12:33,081 --> 00:12:37,080
a far cry from the ditches of previous conflicts.

213
00:12:37,081 --> 00:12:40,080
They intertwined for hundreds of miles.

214
00:12:40,081 --> 00:12:43,620
Frontline trenches, communications trenches,

215
00:12:43,621 --> 00:12:46,920
support trenches, all were tied together

216
00:12:46,921 --> 00:12:50,620
in city-like networks by battlefield engineers.

217
00:12:50,621 --> 00:12:53,620
At the beginning of the war,

218
00:12:53,621 --> 00:12:56,420
generals schooled in classic battlefield techniques

219
00:12:56,421 --> 00:12:59,340
were still accustomed to sending vast waves

220
00:12:59,341 --> 00:13:00,740
of troops at each other.

221
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:05,740
Because of advances in weaponry

222
00:13:05,741 --> 00:13:09,480
and battlefield engineering, these tactics from the past

223
00:13:09,481 --> 00:13:13,820
led to a human slaughter of almost unimaginable proportions,

224
00:13:13,821 --> 00:13:18,320
especially when troops were ordered to attack.

225
00:13:18,321 --> 00:13:21,160
During the Battle of Verdun, one of the bloodiest battles

226
00:13:21,161 --> 00:13:25,960
in the history of the world, it is estimated that 400,000 French soldiers

227
00:13:25,961 --> 00:13:32,800
and 600,000 Germans lost their lives in just six months.

228
00:13:32,801 --> 00:13:36,080
French warfare had to be the worst situation

229
00:13:36,081 --> 00:13:38,700
when one had to eventually go over the top.

230
00:13:38,701 --> 00:13:42,380
And in that case, no man's land must have seemed like an eternity

231
00:13:42,381 --> 00:13:43,940
to reach the other side.

232
00:13:43,941 --> 00:13:46,080
Many people didn't reach the other side.

233
00:13:46,081 --> 00:13:47,800
You had land mines.

234
00:13:47,801 --> 00:13:49,380
You had barbed wire.

235
00:13:49,381 --> 00:13:51,340
Barb wire was there not only to slow you down,

236
00:13:51,341 --> 00:13:54,340
but to literally ensnare you to allow the grim reaper

237
00:13:54,341 --> 00:13:57,360
of the First World War to do its grisly work,

238
00:13:57,361 --> 00:14:00,260
the machine, though, to take out entire units.

239
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:04,860
With miles of barb wire to get across,

240
00:14:04,861 --> 00:14:08,760
battlefield engineers needed the newly introduced tank

241
00:14:08,761 --> 00:14:13,260
to break through the barriers and roll over obstacles.

242
00:14:13,261 --> 00:14:17,000
The tank led to the development of the anti-tank mine,

243
00:14:17,001 --> 00:14:23,520
which in turn led to a need to breach minefields.

244
00:14:23,521 --> 00:14:26,020
Although the full impact of military aircraft

245
00:14:26,021 --> 00:14:28,420
would not be felt until World War II,

246
00:14:28,421 --> 00:14:32,120
their introduction here created a whole new set of problems

247
00:14:32,121 --> 00:14:34,320
and challenges.

248
00:14:34,321 --> 00:14:36,480
Targets far behind the front lines,

249
00:14:36,481 --> 00:14:40,560
which were previously safe, were now vulnerable to attack.

250
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:46,360
And an army's position and movements

251
00:14:46,361 --> 00:14:50,040
were open to surveillance from the air.

252
00:14:50,041 --> 00:14:52,300
Since the days of the Greeks and Romans,

253
00:14:52,301 --> 00:14:56,000
wars were won by the army that could move the fastest.

254
00:14:56,001 --> 00:14:59,920
Motorized warfare took fighting to a new level of speed

255
00:14:59,921 --> 00:15:02,220
and conferred on battlefield engineers

256
00:15:02,221 --> 00:15:04,220
a heightened level of importance.

257
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:07,320
World War I was also important to the engineers

258
00:15:07,321 --> 00:15:10,320
and that it is the point in which the engineers

259
00:15:10,321 --> 00:15:13,320
begin their love affair with the internal combustion engine.

260
00:15:13,321 --> 00:15:16,320
It marks the beginning of the engineers shift

261
00:15:16,321 --> 00:15:21,320
from horse power and manpower to machine power.

262
00:15:21,321 --> 00:15:24,320
That evolution has continued to today.

263
00:15:24,321 --> 00:15:29,320
Without the machinery of today, the engineer's job would be virtually impossible.

264
00:15:29,321 --> 00:15:32,320
Roads that were built to handle horse-drawn carts

265
00:15:32,321 --> 00:15:35,320
carts had to be improved to handle the faster,

266
00:15:35,321 --> 00:15:39,320
heavier gasoline-powered vehicles.

267
00:15:39,321 --> 00:15:42,320
Bridges had to be stronger.

268
00:15:42,321 --> 00:15:44,320
Rail lines had to be repaired.

269
00:15:44,321 --> 00:15:48,320
To a large degree without engineers in the battlefield,

270
00:15:48,321 --> 00:15:52,320
no one could move.

271
00:15:52,321 --> 00:15:55,320
When the United States entered the war in 1917,

272
00:15:55,321 --> 00:15:59,320
the first request from the British and the French governments

273
00:15:59,321 --> 00:16:01,320
was for engineers.

274
00:16:01,321 --> 00:16:03,920
newly organized railway regiments,

275
00:16:03,921 --> 00:16:08,020
and combat engineers rushed to France.

276
00:16:08,021 --> 00:16:14,760
Not surprisingly, the first U.S. casualties were engineers.

277
00:16:14,761 --> 00:16:19,580
This was the war that formalized the concept of fighting engineers.

278
00:16:19,581 --> 00:16:21,980
Prior to this, engineering divisions were separate

279
00:16:21,981 --> 00:16:25,080
from combat units.

280
00:16:25,081 --> 00:16:28,480
Now they were expected to carry shovels and rifles,

281
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:30,820
hammers, and hand grenades.

282
00:16:30,821 --> 00:16:35,820
When American engineers began to arrive in big numbers,

283
00:16:35,821 --> 00:16:38,820
their effect was immediate.

284
00:16:38,821 --> 00:16:40,820
The Germans would blow up the bridges,

285
00:16:40,821 --> 00:16:41,820
but the Americans would rebuild them.

286
00:16:41,821 --> 00:16:44,820
They'd blow up the switches, and the Americans would rebuild them.

287
00:16:44,821 --> 00:16:46,820
They couldn't successfully blow up every foot of track,

288
00:16:46,821 --> 00:16:49,820
so we'd use what was left.

289
00:16:49,821 --> 00:16:54,820
Behind the lines, the engineer's efforts of building ports, roads,

290
00:16:54,821 --> 00:16:59,820
railroads, and support facilities produce some staggering statistics.

291
00:16:59,821 --> 00:17:04,820
The 20th Engineers, the U.S. Army's largest regiment,

292
00:17:04,821 --> 00:17:08,820
helped reduce 200 million board feet of lumber

293
00:17:08,821 --> 00:17:11,820
and 4 million railroad ties.

294
00:17:11,821 --> 00:17:16,820
The scope of the American war effort,

295
00:17:16,821 --> 00:17:20,820
which only lasted a couple of years, was truly amazing.

296
00:17:20,821 --> 00:17:24,820
We brought 2 million men to France, and we had to feed them.

297
00:17:24,821 --> 00:17:26,820
And the engineers were responsible to making sure

298
00:17:26,821 --> 00:17:31,820
that we had fresh American food for these soldiers.

299
00:17:31,821 --> 00:17:35,820
To satisfy a tremendous need for food refrigeration,

300
00:17:35,821 --> 00:17:40,820
one ice-making plant produced 500 tons of ice per day.

301
00:17:40,821 --> 00:17:46,820
Another refrigeration plant could store over 5,000 tons of beef.

302
00:17:46,821 --> 00:17:49,820
American engineers constructed a bakery

303
00:17:49,821 --> 00:17:53,820
that baked 4,000 tons of bread a day.

304
00:17:53,821 --> 00:17:56,820
But the most telling statistic from the

305
00:17:56,821 --> 00:18:00,820
The war involve people, not war supplies.

306
00:18:00,821 --> 00:18:05,820
Experts say 14 million soldiers and civilians may have died.

307
00:18:08,820 --> 00:18:10,820
It would now be up to battlefield engineers

308
00:18:10,821 --> 00:18:14,820
to figure out ways to defend against an even more terrifying

309
00:18:14,821 --> 00:18:17,820
war machine that would soon roll through Europe.

310
00:18:22,820 --> 00:18:26,820
Following World War I, the leaders of France decided to protect

311
00:18:26,821 --> 00:18:32,320
protect the country by constructing an elaborate system of defense.

312
00:18:32,321 --> 00:18:39,940
The Maginot Line was a series of fortifications that stretched 200 miles along the eastern French border.

313
00:18:39,941 --> 00:18:48,340
It consisted of gun emplacements, barracks, and underground command posts, all connected by railroad tunnels.

314
00:18:48,341 --> 00:18:52,720
But as in the past, a new defense spawned a new offense.

315
00:18:56,820 --> 00:19:02,820
The Nazi war machine took the weaponry of the First World War

316
00:19:02,821 --> 00:19:07,820
and updated it into bigger, faster, and deadlier versions.

317
00:19:07,821 --> 00:19:11,820
The heavily mechanized Blitzkrieg outran the French,

318
00:19:11,821 --> 00:19:14,820
and in swiftly storming through Belgium,

319
00:19:14,821 --> 00:19:17,820
went around the heavily fortified Maginot Line.

320
00:19:17,821 --> 00:19:21,820
Once again, the army that could move faster

321
00:19:21,821 --> 00:19:23,820
was the prevailing force.

322
00:19:23,821 --> 00:19:30,820
Newly designed airplanes were equipped with much more powerful machine guns and bombs.

323
00:19:30,821 --> 00:19:38,820
Vastly improved tanks were faster and armed with more killing power.

324
00:19:38,821 --> 00:19:44,820
All of this new technology triggered the stimulus response mechanism of battlefield engineering.

325
00:19:44,821 --> 00:19:46,820
Bombers needed airfields.

326
00:19:46,821 --> 00:19:51,820
Airfields, in turn, were bombed and needed to be rebuilt.

327
00:19:51,821 --> 00:19:55,820
Bridges were blown up and needed to be replaced or repaired.

328
00:19:55,821 --> 00:19:59,820
In response, engineers created prefabricated foot bridges,

329
00:19:59,821 --> 00:20:04,820
treadway bridges, and dummy bridges as decoys.

330
00:20:04,821 --> 00:20:07,820
General Douglas MacArthur, who was himself

331
00:20:07,821 --> 00:20:11,820
at top of his class engineer graduate of West Point,

332
00:20:11,821 --> 00:20:15,820
described World War II as an engineer's war.

333
00:20:15,821 --> 00:20:21,820
When the war broke out, Germany's vast combat engineering superior

334
00:20:21,821 --> 00:20:24,820
seemed to doom every attempt to stop them.

335
00:20:24,821 --> 00:20:27,820
But, as has happened throughout history,

336
00:20:27,821 --> 00:20:31,820
innovations produce counter-innovations,

337
00:20:31,821 --> 00:20:34,820
and it was now up to the Allied battlefield engineers

338
00:20:34,821 --> 00:20:38,820
to respond to this threat in force.

339
00:20:42,820 --> 00:20:45,820
Battlefield engineering will continue in a moment.

340
00:20:45,821 --> 00:20:52,820
We now return to Battlefield Engineering.

341
00:20:52,821 --> 00:21:06,821
The attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941 caused the United States Navy to change the way it built its bases overseas.

342
00:21:09,820 --> 00:21:15,820
Until the surprise strike, the Navy had used civilian engineers and workers.

343
00:21:15,821 --> 00:21:18,320
But shortly after Pearl Harbor,

344
00:21:18,321 --> 00:21:22,320
on Wake Island 2,300 miles southwest of Hawaii,

345
00:21:22,321 --> 00:21:25,820
some civilian workers were captured by the Japanese.

346
00:21:25,821 --> 00:21:29,320
Under the Geneva Convention, these civilians,

347
00:21:29,321 --> 00:21:33,320
if they take up arms, are not protected by any of the covenants

348
00:21:33,321 --> 00:21:36,320
of war and can be executed as spies.

349
00:21:36,321 --> 00:21:38,320
And in a few cases, this actually happened.

350
00:21:38,321 --> 00:21:40,820
Rear Admiral Ben Morel got the go-ahead

351
00:21:40,821 --> 00:21:43,820
to immediately create a naval construction battalion

352
00:21:43,821 --> 00:21:46,320
made up of engineers trained to fight.

353
00:21:46,321 --> 00:21:52,820
The name C-Bs was coined from C-B, the initials for construction battalions.

354
00:21:52,821 --> 00:21:59,820
Their mascot was a fighting bee carrying a machine gun, a wrench, and a hammer.

355
00:21:59,821 --> 00:22:07,820
Mostly our troop, our gang, were from construction companies

356
00:22:07,821 --> 00:22:11,820
that the Navy went through and literally just bought out.

357
00:22:11,821 --> 00:22:17,060
out and uniformed up and sent out.

358
00:22:17,061 --> 00:22:19,660
As a result of looking for engineers and tradesmen

359
00:22:19,661 --> 00:22:23,560
with some experience, the average CB in World War II

360
00:22:23,561 --> 00:22:25,840
was in his 30s.

361
00:22:25,841 --> 00:22:29,940
Many had wives and children and were exempt from the draft,

362
00:22:29,941 --> 00:22:32,740
but volunteered anyway.

363
00:22:32,741 --> 00:22:35,980
The much younger Marines had a standing joke.

364
00:22:35,981 --> 00:22:37,840
Be kind to a CB.

365
00:22:37,841 --> 00:22:39,840
He might be your father.

366
00:22:39,841 --> 00:22:41,820
These old men in their 30s,

367
00:22:41,821 --> 00:22:44,660
were the guys who were going to save your bacon

368
00:22:44,661 --> 00:22:46,620
and do extraordinary things for you,

369
00:22:46,621 --> 00:22:49,460
whether it be in the real sense of the landings

370
00:22:49,461 --> 00:22:51,760
or the quality of life that sometimes

371
00:22:51,761 --> 00:22:54,160
puts you in a whole different situation.

372
00:22:54,161 --> 00:22:56,560
A hot shower, some clean clothes once in a while,

373
00:22:56,561 --> 00:22:59,480
to somebody up to their wasting mud for weeks at a time,

374
00:22:59,481 --> 00:23:01,580
was like heaven.

375
00:23:01,581 --> 00:23:05,080
The challenge of building airstrip after airstrip

376
00:23:05,081 --> 00:23:07,480
was part of the strategy of island hopping.

377
00:23:07,481 --> 00:23:11,480
Taking an island from the Japanese, fortifying it,

378
00:23:11,481 --> 00:23:15,320
and using it as a base of operations to take the next island.

379
00:23:15,321 --> 00:23:18,120
The CBs have to unload their equipment.

380
00:23:18,121 --> 00:23:20,120
They're fighting in a jungle environment.

381
00:23:20,121 --> 00:23:21,620
They're fighting in high humidity.

382
00:23:21,621 --> 00:23:23,320
They're under fire not only on the ground,

383
00:23:23,321 --> 00:23:25,920
but the Japanese are flying overhead and bombing them.

384
00:23:25,921 --> 00:23:29,740
Building an airstrip while having to fight off the enemy

385
00:23:29,741 --> 00:23:34,340
at the same time often created heroic challenges.

386
00:23:34,341 --> 00:23:37,540
Usually a CB would put down his tools and fight,

387
00:23:37,541 --> 00:23:41,280
but Aurelio Tussoni decided to use his bulldozer

388
00:23:41,281 --> 00:23:43,580
as a weapon.

389
00:23:43,581 --> 00:23:44,820
The Japanese were in a pillbox.

390
00:23:44,821 --> 00:23:47,220
They were holding up in advance on an island,

391
00:23:47,221 --> 00:23:49,420
and Arrillo Totsoni drove his bulldozer up

392
00:23:49,421 --> 00:23:52,420
and buried the pillbox, pushed dirt over it,

393
00:23:52,421 --> 00:23:54,720
and simply buried it along with the occupants,

394
00:23:54,721 --> 00:23:57,520
all except for a dog, a Japanese mascot that survived

395
00:23:57,521 --> 00:24:00,340
and was adopted by the battalion.

396
00:24:00,341 --> 00:24:05,340
Another example of CB Heroics involves Captain Wilford Painter.

397
00:24:05,341 --> 00:24:07,440
Captain Painter would come ashore and rubber boat,

398
00:24:07,441 --> 00:24:10,880
face blacked out, all the rest, Tommy Gun, very dramatic.

399
00:24:10,881 --> 00:24:14,720
lay out the U.S. bases on the island while the Japanese are still there.

400
00:24:14,721 --> 00:24:16,380
After he did that, he would leave.

401
00:24:16,381 --> 00:24:17,920
Then the Marines would come and take the island,

402
00:24:17,921 --> 00:24:20,720
and the CBEs would immediately go ashore and build the bases.

403
00:24:20,721 --> 00:24:25,920
But the most famous example of battlefield engineer heroics

404
00:24:25,921 --> 00:24:30,440
must be D-Day, the invasion at Normandy.

405
00:24:34,380 --> 00:24:37,380
Long before the actual invasion of Normandy,

406
00:24:37,381 --> 00:24:40,640
Allied forces were involved in a massive buildup of men

407
00:24:40,641 --> 00:24:42,840
and material.

408
00:24:42,841 --> 00:24:44,980
The Germans had fortified the French coast

409
00:24:44,981 --> 00:24:48,780
with artillery, cement bunkers, barbed wire,

410
00:24:48,781 --> 00:24:51,880
and over a million mines.

411
00:24:51,881 --> 00:24:54,480
The mines posed a serious challenge to the troops

412
00:24:54,481 --> 00:24:56,480
who would have to get through them.

413
00:24:56,481 --> 00:24:58,600
As a commander, I felt very strong

414
00:24:58,601 --> 00:25:04,300
that it was necessary to train people to work

415
00:25:04,301 --> 00:25:08,000
in a dangerous situation.

416
00:25:08,001 --> 00:25:10,640
Creating that pressure-filled situation in a

417
00:25:10,641 --> 00:25:15,640
in advance involved using live booby-trap mines.

418
00:25:15,641 --> 00:25:18,640
One soldier lost his life in the training.

419
00:25:18,641 --> 00:25:20,640
Other soldiers cracked under the strain

420
00:25:20,641 --> 00:25:24,640
of finding and diffusing mines that could blow up at any moment.

421
00:25:24,641 --> 00:25:32,640
When we got through, when it came to question

422
00:25:32,641 --> 00:25:35,640
in France of removing the mines,

423
00:25:35,641 --> 00:25:37,640
we knew who were capable of doing it,

424
00:25:37,641 --> 00:25:40,640
and those people were used, and people

425
00:25:40,641 --> 00:25:46,880
who we found were not able to, were given to other missions.

426
00:25:46,881 --> 00:25:49,880
In addition to heavily fortifying the coastline,

427
00:25:49,881 --> 00:25:52,380
the Germans held all the ports.

428
00:25:52,381 --> 00:25:54,880
An invasion without a harbor to land supplies

429
00:25:54,881 --> 00:26:00,600
would have been not only foolhardy, but suicidal.

430
00:26:00,601 --> 00:26:03,700
The audacious solution of the Allied battlefield engineers

431
00:26:03,701 --> 00:26:08,060
was both highly complex and daringly simple.

432
00:26:08,061 --> 00:26:10,200
It was called Operation Mulberry.

433
00:26:10,201 --> 00:26:13,700
and it involved building a harbor in pieces

434
00:26:13,701 --> 00:26:17,700
and floating it across the English Channel.

435
00:26:17,701 --> 00:26:21,200
Those pieces would be moved using a versatile system

436
00:26:21,201 --> 00:26:26,200
of construction that used five foot by seven foot metal boxes.

437
00:26:26,201 --> 00:26:31,700
What these were was the tried and true pontoon.

438
00:26:31,701 --> 00:26:36,200
In this case, put together seven long, three together

439
00:26:36,201 --> 00:26:39,700
tied here with an outboard engine, and now it becomes a ferry.

440
00:26:39,701 --> 00:26:45,300
a ferry, a flat bed that can carry troops, men, material,

441
00:26:45,301 --> 00:26:47,180
heavy equipment ashore.

442
00:26:47,181 --> 00:26:49,780
You take the same configuration apart with the engine off

443
00:26:49,781 --> 00:26:51,980
and latch it together in a long form,

444
00:26:51,981 --> 00:26:54,480
and you have a causeway or a pier.

445
00:27:00,620 --> 00:27:05,260
On June 6, 1944, the largest amphibious force ever

446
00:27:05,261 --> 00:27:08,020
assembled landed on the beach at Normandy.

447
00:27:09,700 --> 00:27:14,700
Four to five thousand ships, 11,000 planes,

448
00:27:14,701 --> 00:27:19,700
and on the first day, 155,000 troops came ashore.

449
00:27:19,701 --> 00:27:23,700
Immediately before this massive assault,

450
00:27:23,701 --> 00:27:26,700
the combat engineers specially trained

451
00:27:26,701 --> 00:27:29,700
to clear mines snuck ashore in the dark

452
00:27:29,701 --> 00:27:32,700
to clear the way for these troops.

453
00:27:32,701 --> 00:27:38,700
Throughout the attack, the engineers were in the most vulnerable positions.

454
00:27:38,701 --> 00:27:43,700
One of the units lost 67% in wounded or killed.

455
00:27:43,701 --> 00:27:45,700
It's pretty hard to do it.

456
00:27:45,701 --> 00:27:48,700
Sometimes it would have been easier to pick up a rifle

457
00:27:48,701 --> 00:27:51,700
or a machine gun and fire back than to do your job

458
00:27:51,701 --> 00:27:53,700
of clearing the way.

459
00:27:53,701 --> 00:27:56,700
Even as troops were still coming ashore,

460
00:27:56,701 --> 00:28:00,700
engineers began the construction of the Mulberry Harbor.

461
00:28:00,701 --> 00:28:03,700
The Mulberry system was a system of prefabricated dock facilities

462
00:28:03,701 --> 00:28:05,700
that were made of cast concrete,

463
00:28:05,701 --> 00:28:07,700
filled with air and floated across the channel,

464
00:28:07,701 --> 00:28:09,700
and they were sunk right on the Normandy coast

465
00:28:09,701 --> 00:28:12,700
to build our own prefabricated docks.

466
00:28:12,701 --> 00:28:16,700
The first phase involved dynamiting a number of old ships

467
00:28:16,701 --> 00:28:20,700
and then sinking a long line of cement caissons.

468
00:28:20,701 --> 00:28:27,700
Together, the ships and the caissons would form offshore breakwaters.

469
00:28:27,701 --> 00:28:32,700
Next, a floating roadway was built,

470
00:28:32,701 --> 00:28:36,700
stretching from the shore out to the protected area

471
00:28:36,701 --> 00:28:40,200
behind the breakwater.

472
00:28:40,201 --> 00:28:44,200
Once a wharf was constructed at the end of that road,

473
00:28:44,201 --> 00:28:47,700
it allowed the unloading of tanks, trucks, men,

474
00:28:47,701 --> 00:28:50,700
and supplies from larger ships.

475
00:28:50,701 --> 00:28:54,700
In less than two weeks, the harbor was fully functional.

476
00:28:54,701 --> 00:28:59,700
By the end of the month, approximately one million men came ashore.

477
00:28:59,701 --> 00:29:03,700
In addition to building the artificial harbors at Normandy,

478
00:29:03,701 --> 00:29:06,700
the engineers operated the beach camps which housed the shore.

479
00:29:06,701 --> 00:29:07,900
the troops.

480
00:29:07,901 --> 00:29:10,000
They also laid fuel and water lines

481
00:29:10,001 --> 00:29:12,500
and ferried back and forth across the channel

482
00:29:12,501 --> 00:29:15,300
with more men and supplies.

483
00:29:15,301 --> 00:29:19,240
Without the engineers accomplishing all of these tasks successfully,

484
00:29:19,241 --> 00:29:26,260
there could have been no Normandy invasion, no D-Day.

485
00:29:30,260 --> 00:29:33,400
Once Allied troops were on the European mainland,

486
00:29:33,401 --> 00:29:36,600
they faced the difficult task of getting fast-moving tanks,

487
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:38,700
across rivers and streams.

488
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:42,700
British engineers developed the Bailey Bridge,

489
00:29:42,701 --> 00:29:44,700
a portable structure with no single piece

490
00:29:44,701 --> 00:29:47,700
that weighed more than 600 pounds.

491
00:29:48,700 --> 00:29:50,700
It was used when there wasn't enough time

492
00:29:50,701 --> 00:29:52,700
to build a traditional bridge.

493
00:29:53,700 --> 00:29:54,700
In the infantry, you have the term,

494
00:29:54,701 --> 00:29:57,700
whoa, which means, well, let's get going,

495
00:29:57,701 --> 00:29:59,700
let's get the job done.

496
00:29:59,701 --> 00:30:02,700
In the engineers, we have the term layhole heave,

497
00:30:02,701 --> 00:30:05,700
which represents picking up Bailey bridge panels

498
00:30:05,701 --> 00:30:09,700
for construction projects, lay hold, heave, the bridge panel.

499
00:30:09,701 --> 00:30:12,700
And you have soldiers working in unison to build a 10-ton bridge.

500
00:30:12,701 --> 00:30:14,700
It's choreographed like a ballet.

501
00:30:14,701 --> 00:30:17,700
Everyone has to work together and lift and heave together.

502
00:30:17,701 --> 00:30:21,700
Talks about the physical work of the engineers.

503
00:30:21,701 --> 00:30:25,700
The ability to work together to come up with practical solutions

504
00:30:25,701 --> 00:30:29,700
in the field is the common link between the battlefield engineers

505
00:30:29,701 --> 00:30:32,700
of the past 2,500 years.

506
00:30:32,701 --> 00:30:34,700
But as the weapons of war became deadly,

507
00:30:34,701 --> 00:30:37,400
The war became deadlier and more powerful.

508
00:30:37,401 --> 00:30:43,400
The challenge facing the engineers became increasingly more difficult.

509
00:30:43,401 --> 00:30:50,940
Battlefield engineering will be right back.

510
00:30:50,941 --> 00:30:58,200
We now return to Battlefield Engineering.

511
00:30:58,201 --> 00:31:04,640
In 1950, when the Korean War broke out,

512
00:31:04,641 --> 00:31:07,740
battlefield engineers were faced with a demolition job,

513
00:31:07,741 --> 00:31:11,540
breaking through fortifications, breaching minefields,

514
00:31:11,541 --> 00:31:13,240
and blowing up bases.

515
00:31:18,120 --> 00:31:22,560
They were also charged with building vital airfields, bridges, and roads.

516
00:31:22,561 --> 00:31:25,360
In addition, they cleared the jungle

517
00:31:25,361 --> 00:31:28,160
for the mobile Army surgical hospitals.

518
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:31,200
better known as MASH units.

519
00:31:31,201 --> 00:31:36,200
Korea changed dramatically the history of fatalities

520
00:31:36,201 --> 00:31:41,200
because before Korea, 90% of all the wounded

521
00:31:41,201 --> 00:31:44,200
that were hit in the head or the belly died.

522
00:31:44,201 --> 00:31:48,200
And once the MASH were set up, 90% lived

523
00:31:48,201 --> 00:31:52,200
because they could get back to the MASH hospitals.

524
00:31:52,201 --> 00:31:55,200
One of the biggest challenges facing the engineers

525
00:31:55,201 --> 00:31:57,200
was the lack of raw materials to get the job done.

526
00:31:57,201 --> 00:31:59,540
to get the job done.

527
00:31:59,541 --> 00:32:02,040
Cement was not available.

528
00:32:02,041 --> 00:32:04,540
Steel was not available.

529
00:32:04,541 --> 00:32:07,040
Timber was not available.

530
00:32:07,041 --> 00:32:09,040
You must remember that everything south,

531
00:32:09,041 --> 00:32:13,240
roughly everything south of the 38th parallel,

532
00:32:13,241 --> 00:32:17,160
was agriculturally inclined, rice patties.

533
00:32:17,161 --> 00:32:21,560
So it was very innovative engineering.

534
00:32:21,561 --> 00:32:26,160
You couldn't do things precisely.

535
00:32:26,161 --> 00:32:28,460
An example of that innovative engineering

536
00:32:28,461 --> 00:32:31,000
involved figuring out how to get heavy machinery

537
00:32:31,001 --> 00:32:35,100
to operate in sub-zero temperatures.

538
00:32:35,101 --> 00:32:38,680
One company kept their bulldozers warm in warming huts

539
00:32:38,681 --> 00:32:42,840
and then borrowed another idea from the ski slopes.

540
00:32:42,841 --> 00:32:46,540
The problem was once the bulldozer got warm,

541
00:32:46,541 --> 00:32:51,420
the blades got hot and the frozen earth would stick

542
00:32:51,421 --> 00:32:54,080
to the bulldozer blade.

543
00:32:54,081 --> 00:32:56,160
So what we did was,

544
00:32:56,161 --> 00:33:01,060
to radio over to Tokyo and had several thousand pounds

545
00:33:01,061 --> 00:33:05,600
of ski wax that dropped in to our perimeter there,

546
00:33:05,601 --> 00:33:08,240
and we used ski wax on the blades.

547
00:33:08,241 --> 00:33:10,980
And this enabled the blades to work

548
00:33:10,981 --> 00:33:14,040
without the earth freezing onto the blades.

549
00:33:17,440 --> 00:33:21,520
In Korea, because the front line was constantly shifting,

550
00:33:21,521 --> 00:33:24,520
one day's advance was often the next day's retreat.

551
00:33:24,521 --> 00:33:29,860
Ironically, sometimes engineers have to destroy what they just built.

552
00:33:30,860 --> 00:33:34,960
One engineer battalion in Korea built a thousand-foot timber bridge

553
00:33:34,961 --> 00:33:41,360
for tank and logistical resupply, and a week later had to pour diesel and gasoline on it

554
00:33:41,361 --> 00:33:42,840
and burn it.

555
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:47,040
The bridge was ultimately referred to as the Broken Heart Bridge,

556
00:33:47,140 --> 00:33:50,240
and the battalion was referred to later as the Broken Heart Battalion.

557
00:33:51,980 --> 00:33:54,500
Despite valiant individual efforts and innovative efforts and innovative,

558
00:33:54,520 --> 00:33:56,280
innovative engineering.

559
00:33:56,281 --> 00:33:58,160
Broken hearts were the order of the day

560
00:33:58,161 --> 00:34:01,460
for many of the soldiers in the Korean conflict.

561
00:34:01,461 --> 00:34:06,360
And that would also prove to be true of the United States' next big war.

562
00:34:10,360 --> 00:34:13,980
The engineering challenge posed by Vietnam

563
00:34:13,981 --> 00:34:17,480
was that, unlike the other big wars of the 20th century,

564
00:34:17,481 --> 00:34:20,320
there was no front line.

565
00:34:20,321 --> 00:34:22,880
This was compounded by the fact that much of the war

566
00:34:22,881 --> 00:34:24,480
was being fought in a dense,

567
00:34:24,520 --> 00:34:25,220
jungle.

568
00:34:25,221 --> 00:34:31,520
Since this was the first air mobility war, a primary responsibility of the battlefield

569
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:37,940
engineer was to build and maintain airstrips and helicopter landing bases.

570
00:34:37,941 --> 00:34:44,580
Sometimes the engineers to get into difficult areas, jungle areas, built up areas, they

571
00:34:44,581 --> 00:34:46,197
had to helicopter it.

572
00:34:46,198 --> 00:34:51,280
There they would come down the rope ladders with their hand tools,

573
00:34:51,281 --> 00:34:54,500
clear an area for a bigger helicopter to come in, which we'd become.

574
00:34:54,520 --> 00:34:55,760
carrying a bulldozer.

575
00:34:55,780 --> 00:34:59,560
That type of a base could be built by the company of engineers

576
00:34:59,561 --> 00:35:00,460
in about one day.

577
00:35:04,160 --> 00:35:07,560
The almost non-existent infrastructure in Vietnam

578
00:35:07,561 --> 00:35:11,000
meant that engineers were basically starting from scratch,

579
00:35:11,001 --> 00:35:14,100
having to build roads, drainage, and bridges,

580
00:35:14,101 --> 00:35:19,140
and having to do it with almost none of the raw materials required.

581
00:35:19,141 --> 00:35:22,480
What they did have was plenty of mud.

582
00:35:22,481 --> 00:35:24,520
Among our earliest taskings was

583
00:35:24,521 --> 00:35:29,660
the job of getting the Marines up out of the mud so that they could have decent living

584
00:35:29,661 --> 00:35:34,180
accommodations when they were not out in the field searching for the Viet Cong and the North

585
00:35:34,181 --> 00:35:35,015
Vietnamese.

586
00:35:35,016 --> 00:35:40,860
The way we got them out of the mud was to build a what's known as a Southeast

587
00:35:40,861 --> 00:35:43,409
Asia hut for everyone who was there.

588
00:35:43,410 --> 00:35:47,020
We could put in 15, 20, 25 of these an hour once we

589
00:35:47,021 --> 00:35:50,240
got the prefab technique down.

590
00:35:50,241 --> 00:35:54,500
The use of pontoon bridges wasn't really practical because the rivers were being used

591
00:35:54,520 --> 00:35:57,720
for day-to-day navigation.

592
00:35:57,721 --> 00:36:01,820
Instead, conventional cement and steel and timber bridges

593
00:36:01,821 --> 00:36:03,920
had to be constructed.

594
00:36:03,921 --> 00:36:06,360
To counter the overall lack of dockage space,

595
00:36:06,361 --> 00:36:10,500
engineers came up with the DeLong Pier.

596
00:36:10,501 --> 00:36:13,240
Came out of Charleston, South Carolina.

597
00:36:13,241 --> 00:36:19,080
It was a barge that was about 300 feet long by 90 feet wide,

598
00:36:19,081 --> 00:36:22,720
built in Charleston, towed across the water,

599
00:36:22,721 --> 00:36:24,520
to Vietnam, and then set in place.

600
00:36:24,521 --> 00:36:34,520
A team of 15 engineers could put it into position in about 45 days.

601
00:36:34,521 --> 00:36:40,520
To build a timber pier this size would take six months.

602
00:36:40,521 --> 00:36:49,520
Another innovation was the instant air strip, made possible by the use of the T-17 nylon membrane.

603
00:36:49,521 --> 00:36:53,520
Lighter and easier to transport than metal runway sections, it was glued together.

604
00:36:53,521 --> 00:36:58,760
It was glued together over a graded and compacted air strip.

605
00:36:58,761 --> 00:37:02,360
The synthetic material created a surface so strong

606
00:37:02,361 --> 00:37:04,300
it could be used by huge aircraft.

607
00:37:08,260 --> 00:37:10,600
The introduction of the Rome plow,

608
00:37:10,601 --> 00:37:14,040
a rugged bulldozer blade made in Rome, Georgia,

609
00:37:14,041 --> 00:37:16,680
was a tremendous asset in the difficult task

610
00:37:16,681 --> 00:37:19,120
of clearing a jungle filled with mines.

611
00:37:21,040 --> 00:37:23,480
Although the Rome plow could detonate some types

612
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:26,560
of mines without being destroyed itself,

613
00:37:26,561 --> 00:37:31,160
most mines still have to be cleared by engineers on foot

614
00:37:31,161 --> 00:37:35,360
or in vehicles equipped with mine detectors.

615
00:37:35,361 --> 00:37:38,160
It was actually really scary to a certain extent.

616
00:37:38,161 --> 00:37:40,680
You didn't know what was going to happen.

617
00:37:40,681 --> 00:37:44,380
The mine detectors were good and they were bad in the sense

618
00:37:44,381 --> 00:37:47,380
that when you're going down a dirt road in a combat zone,

619
00:37:47,381 --> 00:37:49,980
there's so much shrapnel, there's just all kinds of metal

620
00:37:49,981 --> 00:37:53,520
in the road, so you really had to develop a sense

621
00:37:53,521 --> 00:37:57,120
sense of signal detection to determine whether it was a mine

622
00:37:57,121 --> 00:38:02,120
or something, and you never knew for sure what you were going to find.

623
00:38:02,121 --> 00:38:07,360
In addition to the mines, American engineers had to concern

624
00:38:07,361 --> 00:38:11,980
themselves with the tunnels built by the Viet Cong, engineering

625
00:38:11,981 --> 00:38:14,380
marvels in themselves.

626
00:38:14,381 --> 00:38:18,280
In Coochee province near Saigon, it was estimated that there were

627
00:38:18,281 --> 00:38:21,840
200 miles of tunnels.

628
00:38:21,841 --> 00:38:23,520
Although U.S. forces tried several

629
00:38:23,521 --> 00:38:27,520
techniques to eliminate them, none was completely successful.

630
00:38:27,521 --> 00:38:32,520
The Veyekong tunnel system in some areas was quite extensive.

631
00:38:32,521 --> 00:38:37,520
In some areas, it was even two to three layers deep.

632
00:38:37,521 --> 00:38:41,520
They could put hospital units down there.

633
00:38:41,521 --> 00:38:46,520
They could put maintenance and repair units down into their tunnels.

634
00:38:46,521 --> 00:38:51,520
And they were quite extensive and quite effective.

635
00:38:51,521 --> 00:38:54,320
Whether the United States troops would get the cooperation

636
00:38:54,321 --> 00:38:57,020
of the local people was often a question mark.

637
00:38:58,320 --> 00:39:01,520
But engineers had something extra going for them.

638
00:39:01,521 --> 00:39:06,520
The CB's mission, in addition to supporting the combat troops,

639
00:39:06,521 --> 00:39:10,520
was also to assist the local Vietnamese populace.

640
00:39:10,521 --> 00:39:15,520
And so the roads that we built not only supported the U.S. forces,

641
00:39:15,521 --> 00:39:18,520
they also were frequently of use to the Vietnamese

642
00:39:18,521 --> 00:39:20,520
or the North Vietnamese forces,

643
00:39:20,521 --> 00:39:22,520
and they supported the local economy.

644
00:39:22,620 --> 00:39:27,020
Therefore, we were able to go places

645
00:39:27,120 --> 00:39:31,360
and send single trucks that were marked very clearly

646
00:39:31,460 --> 00:39:36,460
with C.Bs on them in areas that no one else would dare go.

647
00:39:38,880 --> 00:39:42,580
In a war in which few good things are said about the outcome,

648
00:39:42,640 --> 00:39:46,180
the construction projects of the American battlefield engineers

649
00:39:46,220 --> 00:39:50,020
may be the most positive lasting legacy of the conflict.

650
00:39:50,520 --> 00:39:55,520
The lessons learned in the jungles of Southeast Asia

651
00:39:55,521 --> 00:40:02,520
would, some 20 years later, be applied in a vastly different arena,

652
00:40:02,521 --> 00:40:05,520
the deserts of the Middle East.

653
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:13,520
Battlefield engineering will be right back.

654
00:40:13,521 --> 00:40:17,520
We now return to Battlefield Engineering.

655
00:40:17,521 --> 00:40:22,520
We now return to Battlefield Engineering.

656
00:40:22,521 --> 00:40:28,520
When U.S. troops started the build-up in Saudi Arabia,

657
00:40:28,521 --> 00:40:35,520
the challenge posed by a desert environment was poles apart from the muddy jungles of Vietnam.

658
00:40:35,521 --> 00:40:42,520
You have mass formations of armored vehicles, attacking fixed fortifications,

659
00:40:42,521 --> 00:40:45,520
something out of World War II, almost.

660
00:40:45,521 --> 00:40:48,020
Vietnam was a low-intensity war.

661
00:40:48,021 --> 00:40:50,320
It was a brush-fire war.

662
00:40:50,321 --> 00:40:53,520
You had armored vehicles there, not in mass,

663
00:40:53,521 --> 00:40:56,020
going up against fixed enemy positions,

664
00:40:56,021 --> 00:40:58,520
such as we found in the desert.

665
00:40:58,521 --> 00:41:01,520
The desert, as any soldier who has ever fought in it,

666
00:41:01,521 --> 00:41:05,520
will tell you, is an enemy in itself.

667
00:41:05,521 --> 00:41:07,020
The sand was everywhere.

668
00:41:07,021 --> 00:41:08,520
It was kind of like dust storm days.

669
00:41:08,521 --> 00:41:10,520
The tents would be full of sand.

670
00:41:10,521 --> 00:41:14,520
Your gear was, the equipment was, weapons had to be cleaned frequently,

671
00:41:14,521 --> 00:41:17,020
frequently if they would operate.

672
00:41:17,021 --> 00:41:19,020
The challenges were extreme.

673
00:41:19,021 --> 00:41:24,520
One of the extreme challenges was land mines.

674
00:41:24,521 --> 00:41:30,520
This time a new technique was employed to deal with the old problem.

675
00:41:30,521 --> 00:41:35,520
Engineers used a micklick or mine clearing line charge,

676
00:41:35,521 --> 00:41:39,520
a device that fires a hose filled with plastic explosive.

677
00:41:39,521 --> 00:41:43,520
After being launched across the first,

678
00:41:43,521 --> 00:41:47,520
After being launched across a minefield, it was detonated,

679
00:41:49,520 --> 00:41:52,520
creating a safe passageway through the mines.

680
00:41:53,520 --> 00:41:56,520
After months of constructing millions of square feet

681
00:41:56,521 --> 00:42:01,520
of aircraft aprons, camps for tens of thousands of Marines,

682
00:42:01,521 --> 00:42:05,520
and hundreds of acres of ammunition and supply points,

683
00:42:05,521 --> 00:42:10,520
the engineers prepared to support the ground assault into Kuwait.

684
00:42:13,520 --> 00:42:17,020
The most formidable tasks facing the battlefield engineers

685
00:42:17,021 --> 00:42:20,520
was the road network required by General Schwarzkopf's

686
00:42:20,521 --> 00:42:22,520
end-run attack.

687
00:42:23,520 --> 00:42:26,020
It spanned more than 30 miles of desert

688
00:42:26,021 --> 00:42:29,520
and required more than 200 miles of roads.

689
00:42:29,521 --> 00:42:33,520
Some were barely drivable until they were hurriedly improved

690
00:42:33,521 --> 00:42:35,520
just before the attack.

691
00:42:35,521 --> 00:42:39,520
Bottom line is this road was, you know, at the beginning,

692
00:42:39,521 --> 00:42:41,520
maybe you could go five miles an hour.

693
00:42:41,521 --> 00:42:43,520
Towards the end, you could drive 50, 55 miles.

694
00:42:43,521 --> 00:42:46,820
hours' hour on it.

695
00:42:46,821 --> 00:42:49,760
Frankly, when you look at Desert Storm and try to apply the lessons

696
00:42:49,761 --> 00:42:53,060
to the next war, you've got to be careful because we're probably

697
00:42:53,061 --> 00:42:55,160
never going to find that infrastructure again.

698
00:42:55,161 --> 00:42:57,360
And we're probably never going to find an enemy again who says,

699
00:42:57,361 --> 00:43:00,280
I will sit here and wait for you to build up this amazing combat

700
00:43:00,281 --> 00:43:01,280
power.

701
00:43:05,280 --> 00:43:09,280
The future of battlefield engineering is as difficult to predict

702
00:43:09,281 --> 00:43:11,580
as tomorrow's headlines.

703
00:43:11,581 --> 00:43:13,520
But the US Army is now preparing

704
00:43:13,521 --> 00:43:16,960
for adversaries who might use low-tech devices

705
00:43:16,961 --> 00:43:20,560
to combat high-tech weaponry.

706
00:43:20,561 --> 00:43:23,300
To counter this, engineers are developing ways

707
00:43:23,301 --> 00:43:27,940
to deal with what they call an asymmetric threat.

708
00:43:27,941 --> 00:43:31,340
A minor an obstacle is a classic asymmetric threat,

709
00:43:31,341 --> 00:43:34,080
because the enemy can put it in and then leave.

710
00:43:34,081 --> 00:43:35,940
He doesn't have any of his forces at risk,

711
00:43:35,941 --> 00:43:39,040
but when we get there, it slows us down,

712
00:43:39,041 --> 00:43:42,680
takes us out of our plan, or it kills us.

713
00:43:42,681 --> 00:43:47,680
To counter this threat, the Army is developing new strategies.

714
00:43:47,681 --> 00:43:51,980
What we're trying to do in the future is to replace men with machinery,

715
00:43:51,981 --> 00:43:57,920
with mechanization, put fewer soldiers at risk, and do it faster.

716
00:43:57,921 --> 00:44:01,240
One of the weapons systems of the future which exists today

717
00:44:01,241 --> 00:44:05,540
is the M1 Breacher, better known as the Grizzly.

718
00:44:05,541 --> 00:44:08,640
It is equipped with mind-detecting radar and designed

719
00:44:08,641 --> 00:44:12,240
to break through almost any kind of obstacle.

720
00:44:12,241 --> 00:44:15,040
Its most visible feature is a mine clearing blade

721
00:44:15,041 --> 00:44:18,740
that can maintain a steady depth of 15 inches

722
00:44:18,741 --> 00:44:23,780
as it plows a 14-foot wide path through minefields.

723
00:44:23,781 --> 00:44:27,660
The Grizzlies are a replacement for what we today

724
00:44:27,661 --> 00:44:30,500
called the orchestrated ballet of farm implements.

725
00:44:30,501 --> 00:44:32,360
And what we're trying to do with the grizzly

726
00:44:32,361 --> 00:44:35,860
is get a tank platform that can keep up with our forces

727
00:44:35,861 --> 00:44:38,400
and is survivable that can go in and replace

728
00:44:38,401 --> 00:44:40,800
all those soldiers and pieces of equipment.

729
00:44:40,801 --> 00:44:41,800
It makes the job for the commanding.

730
00:44:41,801 --> 00:44:44,600
for the commander on the battlefield much easier,

731
00:44:44,601 --> 00:44:47,180
and it maintains the tempo, because all he has to do

732
00:44:47,181 --> 00:44:50,800
is decide where he wants to do the breach,

733
00:44:50,801 --> 00:44:52,480
slow down the enemy's rate of fire,

734
00:44:52,481 --> 00:44:55,520
and tell the grizzly operators create a lane for me.

735
00:44:57,480 --> 00:45:00,220
The Wolverine consists of a computer-controlled

736
00:45:00,221 --> 00:45:04,360
aluminum bridge on Abrams tank chassis.

737
00:45:04,361 --> 00:45:08,260
The 75-foot bridge only takes four minutes to deploy,

738
00:45:08,261 --> 00:45:10,500
and it can be picked up in less than 10 minutes

739
00:45:10,501 --> 00:45:11,700
for use elsewhere.

740
00:45:11,800 --> 00:45:15,800
An M1 tank weighs about 70 tons.

741
00:45:15,801 --> 00:45:17,800
There are a lot of bridges we can't put it over,

742
00:45:17,801 --> 00:45:19,800
and there are a lot of bridges that our enemies

743
00:45:19,801 --> 00:45:22,800
are going to blow up in front of us to keep us from crossing.

744
00:45:22,801 --> 00:45:26,800
So we need a way to rapidly bridge small gaps.

745
00:45:26,801 --> 00:45:28,800
And the Wolverine gives that to us in a platform

746
00:45:28,801 --> 00:45:30,800
that can keep up with a tank.

747
00:45:33,800 --> 00:45:37,800
To cross much larger gaps of up to 120 feet,

748
00:45:37,801 --> 00:45:40,800
engineers will use this quickly deployed bridge.

749
00:45:40,801 --> 00:45:42,800
With the heavy dry support bridge,

750
00:45:42,801 --> 00:45:45,800
we're going to take the mission that would take a company

751
00:45:45,801 --> 00:45:48,800
of 100 or more soldiers all day to build.

752
00:45:48,801 --> 00:45:51,800
We're going to build that same bridge in about an hour and a half

753
00:45:51,801 --> 00:45:53,800
with 14 people.

754
00:45:53,801 --> 00:45:58,800
It is now the task of battlefield engineers

755
00:45:58,801 --> 00:46:00,800
to do more with less.

756
00:46:00,801 --> 00:46:02,800
But while their numbers may diminish,

757
00:46:02,801 --> 00:46:05,800
their importance seems to grow.

758
00:46:05,801 --> 00:46:09,800
Without the engineer, quite often the bombers would not fly.

759
00:46:09,801 --> 00:46:11,500
bombers would not fly.

760
00:46:11,501 --> 00:46:13,340
The tanks wouldn't cross the river,

761
00:46:13,341 --> 00:46:15,140
and the infantry wouldn't advance.

762
00:46:15,141 --> 00:46:17,140
So the mission 2,000 years ago was the same as it was

763
00:46:17,141 --> 00:46:19,640
in Desert Storm, where we built roads through the desert.

764
00:46:19,641 --> 00:46:22,140
We built bunkers and barracks facilities,

765
00:46:22,141 --> 00:46:24,640
and we brought water to our troops.

766
00:46:24,641 --> 00:46:26,480
So the mission hasn't changed.

767
00:46:26,481 --> 00:46:29,820
The only thing that's changed has been the technology.

768
00:46:29,821 --> 00:46:33,220
In the final analysis, despite the downsizing

769
00:46:33,221 --> 00:46:36,560
of the military, the fact remains that someone will be needed

770
00:46:36,561 --> 00:46:39,700
to operate the machines being used to replace the

771
00:46:39,800 --> 00:46:45,800
If history is any guide, those operators will be battlefield engineers.

772
00:46:45,801 --> 00:46:47,800
engineers.

